[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471/) holds every report about dates as one issue: the parser reads dates
month first. Read by tenant, and by what each report says, the reports show more than one bug.
Each one now has a ticket of its own.

<ProblemTable
  ticket={4471}
  tickets={{
    parser: 'https://github.com/Woitekku/mega-s01e03-what-it-shows/issues/1',
    hint: 'https://github.com/Woitekku/mega-s01e03-what-it-shows/issues/2',
    'one-day': 'https://github.com/Woitekku/mega-s01e03-what-it-shows/issues/3',
  }}
/>

<Callout kind="warn">
Not one fix. Fixing the parser leaves the hint and the one-day moves open.
</Callout>

## `parser` · the shared parser reads dates month first

Tenants who write the day first get their dates back with day and month swapped: "Our 7 April
reminder went out on 4 July." It reaches every day-first tenant, through the app, email and the
booking-v2 form, whose hint already asks for the day first.

Fix: read dates in the tenant's order.

## `hint` · booking-v3 tells day-first tenants to type the month first

booking-v3 prints `MM/DD/YYYY` under its date field. People type what the hint tells them, and the
form reads it the tenant's way, day first: the same swap, from a different bug. Only form reports
from tenants on booking-v3 have it. A parser fix does not reach it.

Fix: print the hint in the tenant's order, or read the field in the order the hint asks for.

## `one-day` · dates move by one day

These reports show no swap. "Invite says Tuesday 12 May. Calendar says Monday 11 May." "Client in
Lisbon got Tuesday. Client in Toronto got Monday. Same meeting." Swapping day and month cannot move
a date by one day; turning a date into a time in one zone and back in another can. The desk noted
them in `desk-notes.json` (`oneDayMove`), and the kb went on counting them under `parser` by tenant.

Fix: find where a booking's date passes through a time zone; keep it a calendar date.

## Why they ended up in one ticket

- Triage merges on the subject line alone ([decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject/)).
  The same subjects come in for every problem, so the subject cannot tell them apart.
- Intake tags a report from the reporter's browser. The cause depends on the tenant: where they
  are and which booking form they run. Compare "tagged as" with "problem" above.
- The ticket export pages overlap, so a raw count of the export runs high.

## Counting

The table is built from `reports-4471.json`, the tenant directory and `desk-notes.json` at render
time, so it follows the desk as reports and notes come in. A report from a tenant the directory
does not know shows up as `unplaced`, without a ticket, until someone files it.

